lem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -3 & 9 \\\\ 1 & -9 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 7 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given a $3\\times 3$ matrix that has precisely $3$ distinct eigenvalues. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(4)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(3)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-2)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247115 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dde3e1c-10d5-545e-9a62-22c85fc1b29a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245005} {:answer/id #uuid "647e3b7e-0ca9-5f38-b5c5-80f3deab8961" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245199} {:answer/id #uuid "af935736-b2a3-5fa2-a6f9-f4c408811dae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245882} {:answer/id #uuid "c02e9581-e491-57d8-ab11-67b2c9547b53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246024} {:answer/id #uuid "ce03609e-f81f-5d97-8559-15581b945101" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 175