h} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245740}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245740} :answer-field/id #uuid "623c533d-6593-5a6e-942c-145a6253af30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246717}] :question/id #uuid "6c1f00e1-65fb-5c7e-8563-e08ab187f6c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-110381" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=0.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given a $3\\times 3$ matrix that has precisely $3$ distinct eigenvalues. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(4)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397985 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ef9491b-f32b-53f6-9519-01ff6e99cf4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397987} {:answer/id #uuid "e04498da-99a5-56ef-87e8-65322c43399a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397988} {:answer/id #uuid "4688b57f-75a4-5e74-8631-0d25866aa18d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397989} {:answer/id #uuid "b2bf4e6