\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412032} {:answer/id #uuid "436fb54f-57b5-5820-9d1c-946aae06a6b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412033} {:answer/id #uuid "80c047ae-9f8f-55d9-8e22-c0abac0118e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412036} {:answer/id #uuid "a9ff8588-b4b4-5b70-8276-5f002a4d8176" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412031} :answer-field/id #uuid "e32bc50b-3a4b-5de0-9dbe-94f5390440cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412048}] :question/id #uuid "c099d5a5-3b8f-5bcf-9f18-6240f88f8abb" :question/math-academy-id "q-110376" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-7.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given a $3\\times 3$ matrix that has precisely $3$ distinct eigenvalues. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(5)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-7)=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks."}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 3x3 Matrix With Three Distinct Eigenvalues"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3115 :topic/title "Jordan Canonical Form of a 3x3 Matrix"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129963} :activity/id #uuid "0e238e7b-3085-5b87-b3c4-0f5c35258e51" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129963 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130347 :tutorial/content "Consider the following $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n![](images/4f/4fd591d8028847aa7e1d11e840acdb192703d152f9c397e40fec98fd1e281c41.svg)\n\nIf we are told that one of the eigenvalues of this matrix is $\\lambda = 3,$ how do we calculate the corresponding eigenvector?\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -7 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this reduced matrix, there are two pivot columns (the $2$nd and $3$rd). Thus, $x_1$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we have $x_3 = 0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain $x_2 = 0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n![](images/70/705dd92c7330897f35671b66866791160b9d7f65e3280d5ccb9d992405c08942.svg)\n\nSetting $x_1=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n![](images/2a/2a5dd6c35fb81134f13e765e0a02be4ea2feedfd878a1923491813492739ef00.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "b1a9d148-15f7-5878-8f36-f19ee99c49d6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3566 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "875dc5aa-e4f4-50f8-a30e-d5ed46d77e32" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129964}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129964 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129127 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c79885-7848-51e3-9f96-6a109e776fd5"} :step/id #uuid "59537f37-7060-5544-9b4a-84501e450c50" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129965}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129965 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129128 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4def2d61-0093-53cf-bcbb-1d2c3e6e1738"} :step/id #uuid "8a926ac2-afd2-5b57-8911-c3b8dac86d39" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130084}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129966 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129129 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e732a2b-a6a1-5e0f-b43e-a9fbc02cfc74"} :step/id #uuid "5d1fcdf4-d676-5ff5-bf31-d7e371f43f08"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130084 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130403 :tutorial/content "Note the following important theorem.\n\n*The set of eigenvectors $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_k \\}$ of a matrix $A,$ where each vector $\\mathbf v_i$ corresponds to a different eigenvalue, is linearly independent.*\n\n**Watch out!** Eigenvectors that correspond to the *same* eigenvalue *can* be (and very often *will* be) linearly dependent." :tutorial/id #uuid "c0f0f6d3-c9e1-52a6-bf51-244bb7751a9d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4432 :tutorial/title "Eigenvectors That Correspond to Different Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "3314b023-f15c-5f8b-a4e4-aeea702377a2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129966}}] :acti